lexicon.ai_affirm (rules.json; RD1's affirms_ai/affirmsAi in both doors) accepts mixed-script and pronoun-less disclosures:
- 'मैं … आपकी AI assistant' and 'Main … ki AI असिस्टेंट' (an assistant word in either script after AI);
- the possessives aapki/aapka/apki;
- one qualifier word ('AI voice assistant', 'AI वॉइस असिस्टेंट');
- a self-introduction with no pronoun ('Riya here, X ki AI assistant'; 'X ki AI assistant Riya bol rahi hoon');
- English apposition ('I am Riya, an AI assistant from X', with a not/never guard in the gap).
The mention guard ('… AI assistant के बारे में') now also covers a Latin 'assistant'. A pre-existing hole had made that mention count as a disclosure, the unsafe direction (the door would skip its line). Lookahead negations end in \b only for Latin words: a \b after a Devanagari word does not hold, and that let '…की AI असिस्टेंट के बारे में' through during the build.

ai_affirm after decision.2026-10-24.ai-affirm-mixed-script. Sandbox, deterministic, TS and Python doors over shared/safety/disclosure.json:
- 15 new affirm vectors (8 true, 7 false: denials, mentions, a third-party AI, 'not an AI assistant');
- 1 new opening vector, the 9b34ff7 staging line, 'discloses: true';
- all earlier affirm and opening vectors hold, in both doors.
Not verified: a staging call after the change.

RD6 rd.amount_not_in_facts (both doors) checks a bare figure against the facts when all of these hold:
- the sentence talks about money (lexicon.money_talk) or uses a money verb (words_to_amounts.bare.money_verb: rahega/रहेगा, bharna/भरना, lagega, banega, dena hoga, kat jayega, 'will be', 'comes to', costs);
- the figure is at least 100;
- what follows is the sentence's end, a money verb or a short particle (bare.follow: hai/ka/tak/per…, never a noun, so '500 hospitals' is a count);
- it is not a year (bare.year), an 8+ digit run, part of a digit run, after an id word (bare.not_before: policy/claim/account/pincode/phone/number…), or before a unit (bare.not_after: %, :, /, baje, tareekh, din, saal, members…).
A multiplied figure ('27 hazaar') with a money verb counts too. The Python KNOWN guard learns words_to_amounts.bare. test.simulate's door (server/modules/agents/simulate.ts) now gets the contact's values as facts, as the runtime's door_ctx does. Before, only the agent's facts were passed, so a contact amount ('premium 18,450') blocked once bare figures were checked. They are not perContact there: the simulation never verifies identity, so RD11 stays out of it, as before.

RD6 bare amounts, sandbox, deterministic, TS and Python doors:
- 19 new cases.json vectors (source dev-bare-amount): 6 harmful blocked (the staging line, Devanagari, Hinglish, English, '27 hazaar rahega') and 13 benign passed (a figure that is in the facts, policy and claim numbers, a year, dates, a phone number, pincode, days, members, '500 hospitals', counts);
- benign agent lines (cases.json benign replies plus dev agent_benign, n = 1,048, empty facts): rd.amount_not_in_facts blocks 5 before and 5 after, the same lines;
- all cases.json vectors hold in both doors.
Not verified: a blind set, or staging.
